{"kind":"stack","id":"s-living-groups","title":"Grouping living things","theme":{"background":"#f4fbf4","foreground":"#1d3b1d","font":"Comic Sans MS, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Living things"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"Things that grow, need food and water, breathe and can reproduce."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Grouping"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"Sorting things into sets that share features, such as animals with backbones or plants with flowers."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Which of these is a living thing?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"A tree","why":"Trees grow, breathe and reproduce, so they are alive.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"A rock","why":"Pupils may think rocks grow because they get bigger as they are added to.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"A cup","why":"Pupils may forget a cup is made by people and never was alive.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"What do most animals with backbones have in common?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"A backbone","why":"Vertebrates are animals with a backbone, which is the feature that groups them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Feathers","why":"Pupils may think all animals with backbones have feathers, but only birds do.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"Six legs","why":"Pupils may mix up backbone animals with insects, which have six legs.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"You find two creatures in the pond. One has a backbone and one does not. Which do you study first?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"The one with a backbone","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"The one without a backbone","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You study the fish first. Its backbone helps sort it into the group of animals with backbones."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"You study the snail first. It has no backbone, so it joins the group of animals without one."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Which plant group has flowers?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Flowering plants","why":"Flowering plants make flowers, which is the feature that puts them in this group.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Mosses","why":"Pupils may think mosses are plants with flowers, but mosses have no flowers.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Ferns","why":"Pupils may think ferns flower like other plants, but ferns reproduce with spores.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"How many groups are these? Cats, dogs, cows and horses are all mammals."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Four","why":"Each animal is named, so four animals share the one group of mammals.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"One","why":"All four animals are mammals, so they belong to one group.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Two","why":"Pupils may split mammals into pets and farm animals, but both are still mammals.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which feature do birds, bats and insects share that helps us group them?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Wings","why":"Birds, bats and insects all have wings, so they can be grouped by this feature.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Fur","why":"Pupils may think all three have fur, but only bats have fur among these.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Scales","why":"Pupils may think birds and insects both have scales, but neither has them as a shared feature.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-t","content":"You finished the grouping living things lesson!"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Well done. Sorting living things helps scientists understand how they are alike and different."}],"actions":[]}}
